Sonic is a simple algorithm for speeding up or slowing down speech.  However,
it's optimized for speed ups of over 2X, unlike previous algorithms for changing
speech rate.  The Sonic library is a very simple ANSI C library that is designed
to easily be integrated into streaming voice applications, like TTS back ends.

The primary motivation behind Sonic is to enable the blind and visually impaired
to improve their productivity with open source speech engines, like espeak.
Sonic can also be used by the sighted.  For example, Sonic can improve the
experience of listening to an audio book on an Android phone.

A native Java port of Sonic is in Sonic.java.  Main.java is a simple example of
how to use Sonic.java.  To play with it, you'll need a "talking.wav" file in the
current directory, and you'll want to change the speed, pitch or other
parameters manually in Main.java, in the main method.

Sonic is Copyright 2010, 2011, Bill Cox, all rights reserved.  It is released
under the Apache 2.0 license, to promote usage as widely as possible.

Performance test:

I sped up a 751958176 byte wav file with sonic (a 9 hour, 28 minute mono audio
file encoded at 16-bit 11.KHz), but with the output writing disabled.  The
reported time, running Ubuntu 11.04 on my HP Pavilion dm4 laptop was:

real    0m50.839s
user    0m47.370s
sys     0m0.620s

The Java version is not much slower.  It reported:

real    0m52.043s
user    0m51.190s
sys     0m0.310s

Update, May 7, 2017
-------------------
I upgraded the pitch change algorithm to use a 12-point sinc FIR filter for
interpolation, rather than linearly interpolating between points.  This
significantly reduces noise introduced by the pitch change algorithm.  It is
most noticable in low-sample-rate streams, such as the 11,025 Hz output of the
Eloquence TTS engine.  The upgrade is in both the C and Java versions.

Speed tracks (variable speed over one stream)
---------------------------------------------
sonicSetSpeedTrack() installs a speed track: a piecewise-linear curve of
playback speed versus position in the original recording.  It is meant for
voice editing, where long pauses should be skipped quickly and speech should
return gradually to normal speed, without splitting the recording into
separate streams.  The sound produced does not depend on the block sizes
used to feed audio into the stream.

Coordinates: each SonicSpeedPoint gives an inputFrame, which is a frame
index into the source PCM stream (one frame is one sample per channel),
counted from 0 at the first frame written after stream creation or after
the most recent sonicFlushStream() call.  The first point must be at frame
0, frames must be strictly increasing and at most 2^53
(SONIC_SPEED_TRACK_MAX_FRAME), and every speed must be a finite value in
[0.5, 2.0].  Between points the speed is linearly interpolated in the
source-frame coordinate; after the last point the last speed is held.  The
library keeps its own copy of the points.  The total output duration is the
integral of 1/speed over the source frames.

Setting restrictions: a track may only be installed before any non-empty
input has been written to the stream, and only when the stream is configured
with pitch = rate = 1.0, quality = 1, a sample rate of 16000 or 48000 Hz,
and 1 or 2 channels.  sonicSetSpeedTrack() returns 1 on success and 0 when
the track is rejected, in which case the previous configuration is kept.
While a track is installed, calls to sonicSetSpeed(), sonicSetPitch(),
sonicSetRate(), sonicSetQuality(), sonicSetSampleRate() and
sonicSetNumChannels() are rejected (the existing values are kept);
sonicSetVolume() keeps working.  Passing an empty track (count = 0) removes
the track and restores the original fixed-speed mode.

Reuse: after sonicFlushStream(), the stream can be reused for the next
segment exactly as with the plain library; the same track is applied to the
new audio starting again at source frame 0.  Silence added by the flush only
drains the buffers and is not part of the source coordinate system.

